6 Discussion: Design implications, Diagnose-then-Compress framework, limitations\n- §7 Conclusion\n- References: 15 cited works\n\n## Related Work Cited\n[1] OpenVLA (Kim et al., 2024)\n[2] π0 (Physical Intelligence, 2024)\n[3] π0.5 (Physical Intelligence, 2025)\n[4] SmolVLA (HuggingFace, 2025)\n[5] X-VLA (2025)\n[6] LIBERO (Liu et al., NeurIPS 2023)\n[7] FastV (Chen et al., ECCV 2024)\n[8] VLA-IAP (2024)\n[9] Compressor-VLA (2025)\n[10] LightVLA (ICRA 2026)\n[11] See What Matters (ICML 2026)\n[12] SpecPrune-VLA (2025)\n[13] Token Merging (Bolya et al., ICLR 2023)\n[14] SparseVLM (2024)\n[15] OpenVLA-OFT (2024)\n\n## Key Experimental Results Summary\n- 6 models span CV from 0.038 (π0, immune) to 0.314 (SmolVLA, catastrophic)\n- SmolVLA: 0% SR at k=0.95 (removing 7/128 tokens)\n- π0.5: L2norm plateau vs random cliff, +36pp gap at k=0.5\n- X-VLA: attention-based pruning 99.2% vs L2norm 42.6% at k=0.5\n- VTR: 0%→60.1% on SmolVLA, 0% on OpenVLA (connector-dependent)\n- Scaling law: Δ(R-L2) monotonically decreasing with CV, |ρ|=1.0\n- OFT vs OpenVLA: same connector → same pruning pattern (action head independence)\n- Perceiver Resampler: fills gap between MLP and pixel-shuffle\n\n## Experiments from Design YAML Not in Paper\n- VTR Long suite (predicted 35.5%, recovery ratio 0.97) — listed as prediction\n- Some OFT cross-suite numbers may be predictions\n- Perceiver Resampler results may be partially predicted\n\n## YOUR TASK\n\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n\n1. **Novelty Assessment**\n   - How novel is the 2-axis framework? Is it truly new or incremental?\n   - Is importance CV as a diagnostic genuinely novel vs. existing work on token importance?\n   - How does VTR compare to existing learned compression methods (Compressor-VLA, Perceiver Resampler, Token Merging)?\n   - Is the attention-based pruning contribution novel given FastV, See What Matters, etc.?\n\n2. **Related Work Completeness**\n   - Are key related works missing? Consider:\n     - Token merging methods (ToMe, etc.)\n     - Vision token compression in VLMs (LLaVA-PruMerge, etc.)\n     - Adaptive token selection methods\n     - Knowledge distillation approaches for VLAs\n     - Other VLA efficiency works\n   - Is the positioning against prior work fair and accurate?\n   - Only 15 references — is this sufficient for a CoRL paper?\n\n3. **Framing and Positioning**\n   - Is the paper positioned as analysis or method? Are expectations set correctly?\n   - Does the 2-axis framework provide actionable insights beyond what practitioners already know?\n   - Is \"Diagnose-then-Compress\" genuinely a framework or just common sense?\n   - Are the claims proportional to the evidence?\n\n4. **Writing Quality**\n   - Is the paper well-structured?\n   - Are figures and tables effective?\n   - Is the narrative clear and compelling?\n\n5. **Significance for the CoRL Community**\n   - Would this change how people build or deploy VLAs?\n   - Is LIBERO-only evaluation sufficient for the claims made?\n   - Sim-to-real gap acknowledged?\n\nProvide scores for: Originality, Quality, Clarity, Significance, Relevance. Then an overall score (1-10) with justification. List 3-5 main strengths and 3-5 main weaknesses. Format as a structured review.","uuid":"72892f49-1a05-4b2f-8588-380d5bed1e2c","session_id":"eee2ebdf-357f-4a4d-85c6-2bc7a2b292e5"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus is **Novelty and Related Work**.\n\n## CoRL Review Criteria\nCoRL evaluates: Originality, Quality, Clarity, Significance, Relevance to robot learning, Limitations. Scale: 1-10 (1-3 reject, 4-5 borderline reject, 6 borderline accept, 7 accept, 8 strong accept, 9-10 outstanding). Acceptance rate ~39%.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Why Token Pruning Crashes Some Robot Policies but Not Others: The Role of Vision Connectors in VLA Token Compressibility\"\n\nThe paper presents a systematic study of vision token pruning across 6 VLA models (π0, π0.5, X-VLA, OpenVLA, OpenVLA-OFT, SmolVLA + RoboFlamingo), 4 connector families (linear, MLP, BART, pixel-shuffle, Perceiver Resampler), and 4 LIBERO task suites. \n\nKey contributions:\n1. 2-axis framework: token coupling (connector type) × task complexity → explains pruning robustness\n2. Importance CV as zero-cost diagnostic (continuous spectrum 0.038→0.314)\n3. CV predicts optimal pruning strategy with |ρ|=1.0 scaling law\n4. VTR (Vision Token Re-encoding) for near-lossless compression on pixel-shuffle connectors\n5. Attention-based pruning rescues entangling connectors where L2-norm fails\n\n## Paper Structure (10 pages)\n- §1 Introduction: Problem framing, contribution list\n- §2 Background and Related Work: VLA models, token pruning for VLMs/VLAs\n- §3 Experimental Setup: 6 models, LIBERO benchmark, pruning protocol, diagnostic metrics, VTR description\n- §4 Results: 9 subsections covering diagnostic spectrum, pixel-shuffle entanglement, MLP graded degradation, π0.5 uniformly critical, task complexity amplification, CV scaling law, attention-based pruning, VTR, Perceiver Resampler\n- §5 Analysis: pixel-shuffle mechanism, entropy vs CV, re-encoding mechanism\n- §6 Discussion: Design implications, Diagnose-then-Compress framework, limitations\n- §7 Conclusion\n- References: 15 cited works\n\n## Related Work Cited\n[1] OpenVLA (Kim et al., 2024)\n[2] π0 (Physical Intelligence, 2024)\n[3] π0.5 (Physical Intelligence, 2025)\n[4] SmolVLA (HuggingFace, 2025)\n[5] X-VLA (2025)\n[6] LIBERO (Liu et al., NeurIPS 2023)\n[7] FastV (Chen et al., ECCV 2024)\n[8] VLA-IAP (2024)\n[9] Compressor-VLA (2025)\n[10] LightVLA (ICRA 2026)\n[11] See What Matters (ICML 2026)\n[12] SpecPrune-VLA (2025)\n[13] Token Merging (Bolya et al., ICLR 2023)\n[14] SparseVLM (2024)\n[15] OpenVLA-OFT (2024)\n\n## Key Experimental Results Summary\n- 6 models span CV from 0.038 (π0, immune) to 0.314 (SmolVLA, catastrophic)\n- SmolVLA: 0% SR at k=0.95 (removing 7/128 tokens)\n- π0.5: L2norm plateau vs random cliff, +36pp gap at k=0.5\n- X-VLA: attention-based pruning 99.2% vs L2norm 42.6% at k=0.5\n- VTR: 0%→60.1% on SmolVLA, 0% on OpenVLA (connector-dependent)\n- Scaling law: Δ(R-L2) monotonically decreasing with CV, |ρ|=1.0\n- OFT vs OpenVLA: same connector → same pruning pattern (action head independence)\n- Perceiver Resampler: fills gap between MLP and pixel-shuffle\n\n## Experiments from Design YAML Not in Paper\n- VTR Long suite (predicted 35.5%, recovery ratio 0.97) — listed as prediction\n- Some OFT cross-suite numbers may be predictions\n- Perceiver Resampler results may be partially predicted\n\n## YOUR TASK\n\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n\n1. **Novelty Assessment**\n   - How novel is the 2-axis framework? Is it truly new or incremental?\n   - Is importance CV as a diagnostic genuinely novel vs. existing work on token importance?\n   - How does VTR compare to existing learned compression methods (Compressor-VLA, Perceiver Resampler, Token Merging)?\n   - Is the attention-based pruning contribution novel given FastV, See What Matters, etc.?\n\n2. **Related Work Completeness**\n   - Are key related works missing? Consider:\n     - Token merging methods (ToMe, etc.)\n     - Vision token compression in VLMs (LLaVA-PruMerge, etc.)\n     - Adaptive token selection methods\n     - Knowledge distillation approaches for VLAs\n     - Other VLA efficiency works\n   - Is the positioning against prior work fair and accurate?\n   - Only 15 references — is this sufficient for a CoRL paper?\n\n3. **Framing and Positioning**\n   - Is the paper positioned as analysis or method? Are expectations set correctly?\n   - Does the 2-axis framework provide actionable insights beyond what practitioners already know?\n   - Is \"Diagnose-then-Compress\" genuinely a framework or just common sense?\n   - Are the claims proportional to the evidence?\n\n4. **Writing Quality**\n   - Is the paper well-structured?\n   - Are figures and tables effective?\n   - Is the narrative clear and compelling?\n\n5. **Significance for the CoRL Community**\n   - Would this change how people build or deploy VLAs?\n   - Is LIBERO-only evaluation sufficient for the claims made?\n   - Sim-to-real gap acknowledged?\n\nProvide scores for: Originality, Quality, Clarity, Significance, Relevance. Then an overall score (1-10) with justification. List 3-5 main strengths and 3-5 main weaknesses. Format as a structured review."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_018gvAdJYRB65WUXyr6vjj4e","session_id":"eee2ebdf-357f-4a4d-85c6-2bc7a2b292e5","uuid":"a46901ea-bdeb-4412-b25e-8815f5258f85","timestamp":"2026-05-25T05:59:40.461Z"}
