{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01RnjqCFppZoSJuc4AQBEY3A","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01AUp3Nsv9YThLt77VqxA9Ds","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Roundtable convergence + synthesis","prompt":"You are the Area Chair moderating a roundtable convergence between two reviewers for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your job is to identify areas of agreement/disagreement, resolve scoring differences with justified reasoning, and produce a final consolidated assessment.\n\n## Paper\n\"Why Token Pruning Crashes Some Robot Policies but Not Others: The Role of Vision Connectors in VLA Token Compressibility\" — CoRL 2026 submission studying VLA token pruning across 6 models, 4 connector families, 4 LIBERO suites.\n\n## R1 Review (Methods + Experiments) — Score: 5/10 (Borderline Reject)\nScores: Originality=7, Quality=5, Clarity=7, Significance=6, Relevance=8\n\n### R1 Strengths:\n1. First cross-architecture VLA pruning study\n2. SmolVLA pixel-shuffle finding is definitive\n3. Double dissociation in scoring methods is clean and actionable\n4. Honest limitations, extensive appendix\n5. CV as simple diagnostic is practical\n\n### R1 Weaknesses:\n1. Incomplete data matrix — OpenVLA evaluated on Object suite only, many \"--\" entries\n2. Single-seed dominance — most conditions seed 42 only; \"scaling law\" fits 3 points\n3. Confounded comparisons — 6 models differ in backbone/scale/training/connector simultaneously; only OFT-OpenVLA is controlled\n4. No head-to-head with concurrent VLA pruning methods (VLA-IAP, LightVLA, etc.)\n5. VTR validated on one model (SmolVLA), failed on another (OpenVLA)\n\n### R1 Key Concerns:\n- Pruning protocol re-indexes positions → cannot separate token removal from positional disruption\n- OFT and π0 evaluated offline only → counting 6 architectures overstates coverage\n- VTR training uses batch 4 vs official 512, weakened baseline (44.8% vs 59.2%)\n- Attention-based pruning cost not discussed (extra forward pass vs \"zero-cost\" CV)\n- Hypergeometric cliff model post-hoc fit to 3 points\n\n---\n\n## R2 Review (Novelty + Related Work) — Score: 6/10 (Borderline Accept)\nScores: Originality=5, Quality=7, Clarity=7, Significance=6, Relevance=8\n\n### R2 Strengths:\n1. First systematic cross-architecture VLA token pruning study fills genuine gap\n2. Double dissociation is clean, surprising, actionable\n3. SmolVLA pixel-shuffle cliff well-validated and mechanistically clear\n4. Honest limitations with appropriate hedging\n5. Extensive appendix with reproducibility detail\n\n### R2 Weaknesses:\n1. Limited novelty in individual contributions — CV basic, VTR ≈ Q-Former, attention scoring extends FastV\n2. Incomplete experimental coverage — OpenVLA Object-only, main table has gaps\n3. CV diagnostic validated on too few models (3-4 data points)\n4. LIBERO-only, no real-robot or cross-benchmark\n5. VTR weak and potentially confounded (batch size difference, extended training)\n\n### R2 Key Concerns:\n- VTR is architecturally identical to Q-Former/Perceiver Resampler — low methodological novelty\n- Paper positioned as analysis+method but VTR contribution is weak; should lean into pure analysis\n- \"Diagnose-then-Compress\" is essentially common sense, not a framework\n- Missing related work discussion: knowledge distillation, temporal compression, broader VLM compression\n- \"20x amplification\" framing is fragile to base-rate sensitivity\n\n---\n\n## Areas of Clear Agreement (both reviewers):\n1. Paper fills a genuine gap — first cross-architecture VLA pruning study\n2. SmolVLA pixel-shuffle finding is the strongest result\n3. Double dissociation (attention vs L2 × connector type) is the most compelling/actionable result\n4. Incomplete data matrix is a real problem (OpenVLA Object-only)\n5. LIBERO-only limits generalizability\n6. VTR is narrowly validated\n7. CV diagnostic on too few models\n8. Relevance to CoRL is high (8/10 from both)\n9. Clarity is good (7/10 from both)\n\n## Areas of Disagreement:\n1. **Originality**: R1=7 (\"first systematic study, 2-axis framework novel\"), R2=5 (\"individual components not novel, empirical-only novelty\")\n2. **Quality**: R1=5 (\"incomplete matrix, single-seed, confounded\"), R2=7 (\"thorough within scope, appropriate stats\")\n3. **Overall**: R1=5 (borderline reject), R2=6 (borderline accept)\n\n---\n\n## YOUR TASK\n\n1. **Resolve the Originality disagreement (R1=7 vs R2=5)**: Is the novelty primarily in the empirical synthesis (R2's view) or does the systematic framework constitute genuine novelty (R1's view)? Consider what CoRL values — is an empirical analysis paper with clear practical implications novel enough?\n\n2. **Resolve the Quality disagreement (R1=5 vs R2=7)**: R1 emphasizes gaps (missing data, single seed, confounds) while R2 emphasizes thoroughness within scope. Which framing is more appropriate for the claims made?\n\n3. **Determine the overall score**: Given CoRL's ~39% acceptance rate, should this paper be accepted? Consider:\n   - The paper's primary contribution is empirical analysis, not a new method\n   - The findings are practically useful but limited to simulation\n   - The experimental coverage has real gaps but covers more ground than any prior work\n   - No concurrent VLA pruning methods are compared head-to-head\n\n4. **Produce the final consolidated review report** in this format:\n\n```\n# Dream Review Report — [Paper Title]\n\n## Venue: CoRL 2026\n\n## Paper Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n## Consolidated Scores\n| Criterion | R1 | R2 | Final | Justification |\n|-----------|----|----|-------|---------------|\n| Originality | | | | |\n| Quality | | | | |\n| Clarity | | | | |\n| Significance | | | | |\n| Relevance | | | | |\n\n## Overall Score: X/10 — [Label]\n\n## Roundtable Consensus\n\n### Key Agreements\n[bulleted]\n\n### Resolved Disagreements  \n[describe how each was resolved]\n\n## Strengths (ranked)\n1. ...\n2. ...\n3. ...\n4. ...\